/-!
# The permutation model of `M₂₂`, its order, and the surjection from its presented group

This file defines the permutation model `TauCeti.Sporadic.M22` of the Mathieu group `M₂₂`: the
subgroup of `Equiv.Perm (Fin 22)` generated by the two permutations `M22.a` and `M22.b` of the
ATLAS files `M22G1-p22B0.m1` and `M22G1-p22B0.m2`. The files list the images of the points
`1, …, 22`; the point `i` of a file is the point `i - 1` of `Fin 22`, and each generator is written
as the product of its disjoint cycles in Mathlib's cycle notation. In the files' numbering,

```text
a = (1,13)(2,8)(3,16)(4,12)(6,22)(7,17)(9,10)(11,14),
b = (1,22,3,21)(2,18,4,13)(5,12)(6,11,7,15)(8,14,20,10)(17,19).
```

So `a` is an involution with six fixed points and `b` has order `4` with two fixed points; the
orders `2`, `4` and `11` of `a`, `b` and `a * b` are recorded as a check on the transcription.
Reading the files as preimages instead of images would replace `b` by `b⁻¹`, which sends `0` to
`20` rather than `21`.

It then shows that the presentation of `M₂₂` carried by `TauCeti.SporadicName.presentation`
(Conder–Havas–Ramsay, relators `a⁴ b A b A b`, `a² b A B a b² A B` and `b¹¹`) holds in the model.
Its generators `a` and `b` are sent to

```text
M22.imageA = c[0, 9, 1, 17, 19] * c[3, 7, 11, 6, 20] * c[4, 18, 15, 14, 21] * c[8, 13, 10, 16, 12]
M22.imageB = c[0, 5, 13, 19, 8, 9, 1, 17, 11, 4, 3] * c[2, 20, 12, 7, 10, 16, 18, 6, 14, 21, 15]
```

of orders `5` and `11`, on which all three transcribed relators evaluate to `1`, with products
composed as in Mathlib, `(σ * τ) i = σ (τ i)`. These two permutations generate the model:
`M22.imageA` and `M22.imageB = a * b` are words in the ATLAS generators, and the ATLAS generators
are words in `M22.imageA` and `M22.imageB`. Extending the assignment therefore gives a
homomorphism from `TauCeti.SporadicName.M22.Group` whose range is exactly the model, hence a
surjection onto it; in particular the presented group is nontrivial.

Finally it computes the order of the model, `443520`. Along the base `0, 1, 2, 3, 4` the model has
basic orbits of lengths `22`, `21`, `20`, `16` and `3`: for each `i < 5` an explicit list of
elements of the model fixes `0, …, i - 1` and sends `i` to each point of its basic orbit. The two
generators, and every product `s * r` of an element `s` of the `j`-th list with an element `r` of
the `i`-th list for `i ≤ j`, sift through these lists from the `i`-th on, which by
`Subgroup.natCard_eq_prod_length_of_siftCheck` pins the order to the product of their lengths. The
sifts are run by `decide` in the kernel. To keep them cheap, every listed element is a product of at
most three of ten permutations given by tables of images: the ATLAS generators and eight further
elements of the model, each checked to be a word in the ATLAS generators. The same argument shows
that `0, 1, 2, 3, 4` is a base: only the identity of the model fixes these five points. Through the
surjection, the presented group has at least `443520` elements whenever it is finite.

## Main declarations

* `TauCeti.Sporadic.M22`: the permutation model of `M₂₂` on `22` points.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.M22.presentationImage`: the images of the presentation generators.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.M22.lift_presentationImage_toFreeGroup_eq_one`: every transcribed relator of
  the presentation of `M₂₂` evaluates to `1` at these images.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.M22.closure_range_presentationImage`: the images generate the model.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.M22.presentationHom`: the homomorphism from the presented group.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.exists_surjective_M22`: the presented group maps onto the model.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.card_M22`: the model has `443520` elements.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.M22.stabilizerChain_five_eq_bot`: the points `0, 1, 2, 3, 4` form a base.
* `TauCeti.Sporadic.le_natCard_group_M22`: the presented group, when finite, has at least
  `443520` elements.

## References

* R. A. Wilson, R. A. Parker, J. N. Bray et al., *ATLAS of Finite Group Representations*,
  version 3, representation `M22G1-p22B0`, <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/>; generator
  image files <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/spor/M22/mtx/M22G1-p22B0.m1> and
  <https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/spor/M22/mtx/M22G1-p22B0.m2>.
* M. D. E. Conder, G. Havas and C. Ramsay, *Efficient presentations for the Mathieu simple group
  M22 and its cover*, in Finite Geometries, Groups, and Computation, Walter de Gruyter, Berlin,
  2006, 33–41, Section 3.4.
* Á. Seress, *Permutation Group Algorithms*, Cambridge Tracts in Mathematics 152, Cambridge
  University Press, 2003, §4.1.
-/

public section

namespace TauCeti.Sporadic

/-- The first generator of the permutation model of `M₂₂`, from the ATLAS file `M22G1-p22B0.m1`:
the involution `(1,13)(2,8)(3,16)(4,12)(6,22)(7,17)(9,10)(11,14)` of the file, shifted to the
points `0, …, 21`. -/
def M22.a : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) :=
  c[0, 12] * c[1, 7] * c[2, 15] * c[3, 11] * c[5, 21] * c[6, 16] * c[8, 9] * c[10, 13]

/-- The second generator of the permutation model of `M₂₂`, from the ATLAS file `M22G1-p22B0.m2`:
the permutation `(1,22,3,21)(2,18,4,13)(5,12)(6,11,7,15)(8,14,20,10)(17,19)` of order `4` of the
file, shifted to the points `0, …, 21`. -/
def M22.b : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) :=
  c[0, 21, 2, 20] * c[1, 17, 3, 12] * c[4, 11] * c[5, 10, 6, 14] * c[7, 13, 19, 9] * c[16, 18]

/-- The permutation model of the Mathieu group `M₂₂`: the subgroup of `Equiv.Perm (Fin 22)`
generated by the permutations `M22.a` and `M22.b` of the ATLAS files `M22G1-p22B0.m1` and
`M22G1-p22B0.m2`. -/
def M22 : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm (Fin 22)) := Subgroup.closure {M22.a, M22.b}

namespace M22

theorem a_def :
    a = c[0, 12] * c[1, 7] * c[2, 15] * c[3, 11] * c[5, 21] * c[6, 16] * c[8, 9] * c[10, 13] :=
  (rfl)

theorem b_def :
    b = c[0, 21, 2, 20] * c[1, 17, 3, 12] * c[4, 11] * c[5, 10, 6, 14] * c[7, 13, 19, 9] *
      c[16, 18] :=
  (rfl)

/-- The permutation model `M22` is the subgroup generated by the ATLAS generators. This is the
unfolding lemma for `TauCeti.Sporadic.M22`, whose body is not exposed. -/
theorem M22_def : M22 = Subgroup.closure {a, b} := (rfl)

theorem a_mem : a ∈ M22 := Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp)

theorem b_mem : b ∈ M22 := Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp)

/-! ### Generator orders -/

/- The equations between explicit permutations of `Fin 22` in this file are closed by
`decide +kernel`: the elaborator's `decide` exhausts its recursion depth while evaluating
`Cycle.formPerm`, while the kernel checks each of them directly. -/

theorem orderOf_a : orderOf a = 2 :=
  orderOf_eq_prime (by decide +kernel) (by decide +kernel)

theorem orderOf_b : orderOf b = 4 :=
  orderOf_eq_prime_pow (p := 2) (n := 1) (by decide +kernel) (by decide +kernel)

theorem orderOf_a_mul_b : orderOf (a * b) = 11 :=
  have : Fact (Nat.Prime 11) := ⟨by decide⟩
  orderOf_eq_prime (by decide +kernel) (by decide +kernel)

/-! ### The presentation of `M₂₂` holds in the model -/

/-- The image in the permutation model of the generator `a` (index `0`) of the presentation of
`M₂₂`: the permutation `c[0, 9, 1, 17, 19] * c[3, 7, 11, 6, 20] * c[4, 18, 15, 14, 21] *
c[8, 13, 10, 16, 12]` of order `5`. -/
def imageA : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) :=
  c[0, 9, 1, 17, 19] * c[3, 7, 11, 6, 20] * c[4, 18, 15, 14, 21] * c[8, 13, 10, 16, 12]

/-- The image in the permutation model of the generator `b` (index `1`) of the presentation of
`M₂₂`: the permutation `c[0, 5, 13, 19, 8, 9, 1, 17, 11, 4, 3] *
c[2, 20, 12, 7, 10, 16, 18, 6, 14, 21, 15]` of order `11`, which is `a * b`. -/
def imageB : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) :=
  c[0, 5, 13, 19, 8, 9, 1, 17, 11, 4, 3] * c[2, 20, 12, 7, 10, 16, 18, 6, 14, 21, 15]

theorem imageA_def :
    imageA = c[0, 9, 1, 17, 19] * c[3, 7, 11, 6, 20] * c[4, 18, 15, 14, 21] *
      c[8, 13, 10, 16, 12] :=
  (rfl)

theorem imageB_def :
    imageB = c[0, 5, 13, 19, 8, 9, 1, 17, 11, 4, 3] * c[2, 20, 12, 7, 10, 16, 18, 6, 14, 21, 15] :=
  (rfl)

/-- The images in the permutation model of the generators of the presentation of `M₂₂`: `imageA`
for the generator of index `0` and `imageB` for the generator of index `1`. -/
def presentationImage (i : Fin SporadicName.M22.presentation.generatorCount) :
    Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) :=
  if (i : ℕ) = 0 then imageA else imageB

@[simp]
theorem presentationImage_zero (h : 0 < SporadicName.M22.presentation.generatorCount) :
    presentationImage ⟨0, h⟩ = imageA := by
  simp [presentationImage]

@[simp]
theorem presentationImage_one (h : 1 < SporadicName.M22.presentation.generatorCount) :
    presentationImage ⟨1, h⟩ = imageB := by
  simp [presentationImage]

theorem range_presentationImage : Set.range presentationImage = {imageA, imageB} := by
  have h : SporadicName.M22.presentation.generatorCount = 2 := by simp
  ext σ
  simp only [Set.mem_range, Set.mem_insert_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨i, rfl⟩
    by_cases hi : (i : ℕ) = 0 <;> simp [presentationImage, hi]
  · rintro (rfl | rfl)
    · exact ⟨⟨0, by omega⟩, presentationImage_zero _⟩
    · exact ⟨⟨1, by omega⟩, presentationImage_one _⟩

/-- **The relators of `M₂₂` hold in the model.** All three transcribed relators of the
presentation of `M₂₂` evaluate to `1` at `presentationImage`. -/
theorem lift_presentationImage_toFreeGroup_eq_one :
    ∀ t ∈ SporadicName.M22.presentation.transcribed,
      FreeGroup.lift presentationImage t.toFreeGroup = 1 := by
  -- The type of `presentationImage` mentions `SporadicName.M22.presentation`, so the statement is
  -- first abstracted over the presentation and then specialized along `presentation_M22`.
  suffices key : ∀ P : GroupPresentation, P = m22Presentation → ∀ t ∈ P.transcribed,
      FreeGroup.lift (fun i : Fin P.generatorCount ↦ if (i : ℕ) = 0 then imageA else imageB)
        t.toFreeGroup = 1 from
    key _ SporadicName.presentation_M22
  rintro P rfl
  simp only [m22Presentation_transcribed, List.mem_cons, List.not_mem_nil, or_false,
    forall_eq_or_imp, forall_eq, Relator.lift_toFreeGroup, Relator.eval_mul, Relator.eval_pow,
    Relator.eval_inv, Relator.eval_gen, one_ne_zero, ↓reduceIte]
  decide +kernel

/-- `imageA` as a word in the ATLAS generators. -/
theorem imageA_eq :
    imageA = b * a * b⁻¹ * a * b⁻¹ * a * b⁻¹ * a * b⁻¹ * a * b * b * a * b * a * b⁻¹ := by
  decide +kernel

/-- `imageB` is the product `a * b` of the ATLAS generators. -/
theorem imageB_eq : imageB = a * b := by
  decide +kernel

/-- The ATLAS generator `a` as a word in the images of the presentation generators. -/
theorem a_eq :
    a = imageA⁻¹ * imageB⁻¹ * imageA⁻¹ * imageB * imageA⁻¹ * imageA⁻¹ * imageB * imageA *
      imageA := by
  decide +kernel

/-- The ATLAS generator `b` as a word in the images of the presentation generators. -/
theorem b_eq : b = a * imageB := by
  decide +kernel

/-- **The images generate the model.** The subgroup generated by the images of the presentation
generators is the permutation model `M22`. -/
theorem closure_range_presentationImage :
    Subgroup.closure (Set.range presentationImage) = M22 := by
  rw [range_presentationImage]
  refine le_antisymm (Subgroup.closure_le _ |>.2 ?_) (Subgroup.closure_le _ |>.2 ?_)
  · have ha := a_mem
    have hb := b_mem
    rintro σ (rfl | rfl) <;>
    · simp only [SetLike.mem_coe, imageA_eq, imageB_eq, mul_mem_cancel_right ha,
        mul_mem_cancel_right hb, mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hb), ha, hb]
  · have hA : imageA ∈ Subgroup.closure {imageA, imageB} := Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp)
    have hB : imageB ∈ Subgroup.closure {imageA, imageB} := Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp)
    have ha : a ∈ Subgroup.closure {imageA, imageB} := by
      simp only [a_eq, mul_mem_cancel_right hA, mul_mem_cancel_right hB,
        mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hA), mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hB), inv_mem hA]
    rintro σ (rfl | rfl)
    · exact ha
    · simpa only [SetLike.mem_coe, b_eq] using mul_mem ha hB

/-- The homomorphism from the group presented by the presentation of `M₂₂` to
`Equiv.Perm (Fin 22)` sending the presentation generators to `presentationImage`. -/
def presentationHom : SporadicName.M22.Group →* Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) :=
  SporadicName.M22.presentation.homEquiv.symm
    ⟨presentationImage, lift_presentationImage_toFreeGroup_eq_one⟩

@[simp]
theorem presentationHom_of (i : Fin SporadicName.M22.presentation.generatorCount) :
    presentationHom (PresentedGroup.of i) = presentationImage i :=
  GroupPresentation.homEquiv_symm_apply_of _ _ i

/-- The range of `presentationHom` is the permutation model `M22`. -/
theorem range_presentationHom : presentationHom.range = M22 :=
  (GroupPresentation.range_homEquiv_symm _ _).trans closure_range_presentationImage

end M22

/-- **The presented group of `M₂₂` maps onto the permutation model.** The group defined by the
presentation of `M₂₂` carried by `TauCeti.SporadicName.presentation` has a surjective homomorphism
onto `M22`. -/
theorem exists_surjective_M22 :
    ∃ f : SporadicName.M22.Group →* M22, Function.Surjective f :=
  SporadicName.M22.presentation.exists_surjective_of_closure_range_eq
    ⟨M22.presentationImage, M22.lift_presentationImage_toFreeGroup_eq_one⟩
    M22.closure_range_presentationImage

/-- The permutation model `M22` is nontrivial: it contains the involution `M22.a`. -/
instance : Nontrivial M22 :=
  M22.nontrivial_iff_exists_ne_one.2 ⟨M22.a, M22.a_mem, by decide +kernel⟩

/-- The group presented by the presentation of `M₂₂` is nontrivial, since it maps onto the
nontrivial permutation model `M22`. -/
instance : Nontrivial SporadicName.M22.Group :=
  exists_surjective_M22.choose_spec.nontrivial

/-! ### The order of the model -/

namespace M22

/- The kernel evaluates a permutation given by its tables of images and inverse images much faster
than a product of cycles or a long word, so the sifting computations below run on such tables: the
two ATLAS generators, and eight further elements of `M22` fixing `0`, `0, 1`, `0, 1, 2` or
`0, 1, 2, 3`. Each of the latter is checked once to be a word in the ATLAS generators, and the
representatives along the base are products of at most three of these ten tables and their
inverses, so that sifting stays cheap. -/

/-- The generator `M22.a`, given by its table of images; it is an involution. -/
private def aTable : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![12, 7, 15, 11, 4, 21, 16, 1, 9, 8, 13, 3, 0, 10, 14, 2, 6, 17, 18, 19, 20, 5]
  invFun := ![12, 7, 15, 11, 4, 21, 16, 1, 9, 8, 13, 3, 0, 10, 14, 2, 6, 17, 18, 19, 20, 5]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- The generator `M22.b`, given by its tables of images and inverse images. -/
private def bTable : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![21, 17, 20, 12, 11, 10, 14, 13, 8, 7, 6, 4, 1, 19, 5, 15, 18, 3, 16, 9, 0, 2]
  invFun := ![20, 12, 21, 17, 11, 14, 10, 9, 8, 19, 5, 4, 3, 7, 6, 15, 18, 1, 16, 13, 2, 0]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- An involution fixing `0`, given by its table of images; by `g1_eq` it is a word in the ATLAS
generators. -/
private def g1 : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![0, 21, 10, 3, 12, 5, 19, 8, 7, 9, 2, 11, 4, 17, 18, 20, 16, 13, 14, 6, 15, 1]
  invFun := ![0, 21, 10, 3, 12, 5, 19, 8, 7, 9, 2, 11, 4, 17, 18, 20, 16, 13, 14, 6, 15, 1]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- A permutation fixing `0`, given by its tables of images and inverse images; by `h1_eq` it is a
word in the ATLAS generators. -/
private def h1 : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![0, 17, 7, 5, 13, 16, 21, 19, 1, 11, 4, 3, 10, 20, 14, 8, 9, 18, 15, 6, 12, 2]
  invFun := ![0, 8, 21, 11, 10, 3, 19, 2, 15, 16, 12, 9, 20, 4, 14, 18, 5, 1, 17, 7, 13, 6]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- A permutation fixing `0, 1`, given by its tables of images and inverse images; by `g2_eq` it
is a word in the ATLAS generators. -/
private def g2 : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![0, 1, 13, 14, 9, 17, 20, 11, 15, 5, 7, 21, 8, 19, 3, 16, 12, 4, 2, 18, 6, 10]
  invFun := ![0, 1, 18, 14, 17, 9, 20, 10, 12, 4, 21, 7, 16, 2, 3, 8, 15, 5, 19, 13, 6, 11]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- A permutation fixing `0, 1`, given by its tables of images and inverse images; by `h2_eq` it
is a word in the ATLAS generators. -/
private def h2 : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![0, 1, 10, 13, 2, 17, 4, 6, 3, 15, 7, 16, 21, 9, 5, 8, 20, 12, 19, 11, 18, 14]
  invFun := ![0, 1, 4, 8, 6, 14, 7, 10, 15, 13, 2, 19, 17, 3, 21, 9, 11, 5, 20, 18, 16, 12]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- An involution fixing `0, 1, 2`, given by its table of images; by `g3_eq` it is a word in the
ATLAS generators. -/
private def g3 : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![0, 1, 2, 14, 5, 4, 20, 16, 21, 9, 12, 15, 10, 18, 3, 11, 7, 17, 13, 19, 6, 8]
  invFun := ![0, 1, 2, 14, 5, 4, 20, 16, 21, 9, 12, 15, 10, 18, 3, 11, 7, 17, 13, 19, 6, 8]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- A permutation fixing `0, 1, 2`, given by its tables of images and inverse images; by `h3_eq`
it is a word in the ATLAS generators. -/
private def h3 : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![0, 1, 2, 8, 20, 14, 16, 5, 18, 19, 12, 11, 15, 6, 7, 10, 13, 9, 3, 17, 21, 4]
  invFun := ![0, 1, 2, 18, 21, 7, 13, 14, 3, 17, 15, 11, 10, 16, 5, 12, 6, 19, 8, 9, 4, 20]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- A permutation fixing `0, 1, 2`, given by its tables of images and inverse images; by `k3_eq`
it is a word in the ATLAS generators. -/
private def k3 : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![0, 1, 2, 10, 20, 3, 12, 16, 7, 17, 5, 4, 18, 14, 15, 13, 8, 19, 6, 9, 11, 21]
  invFun := ![0, 1, 2, 5, 11, 10, 18, 8, 16, 19, 3, 20, 6, 15, 13, 14, 7, 9, 12, 17, 4, 21]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

/-- A permutation fixing `0, 1, 2, 3`, given by its tables of images and inverse images; by
`g4_eq` it is a word in the ATLAS generators. -/
private def g4 : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22) where
  toFun := ![0, 1, 2, 3, 15, 10, 14, 13, 18, 17, 21, 8, 7, 12, 20, 16, 4, 19, 11, 9, 6, 5]
  invFun := ![0, 1, 2, 3, 16, 21, 20, 12, 11, 19, 5, 18, 13, 7, 6, 4, 15, 9, 8, 17, 14, 10]
  left_inv := by decide
  right_inv := by decide

private theorem g1_eq : g1 = bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem h1_eq :
    h1 = aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem g2_eq :
    g2 = aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable *
      bTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem h2_eq :
    h2 = aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * bTable * aTable *
      bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable * aTable := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem g3_eq :
    g3 = bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable *
      bTable * bTable * aTable * bTable := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem h3_eq :
    h3 = aTable * bTable * bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ *
      aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable *
      bTable⁻¹ := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem k3_eq :
    k3 = bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable *
      bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable * bTable * aTable * bTable *
      bTable := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem g4_eq :
    g4 = aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable *
      bTable * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable * aTable * bTable * bTable * aTable * bTable *
      aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable⁻¹ * aTable * bTable := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem aTable_eq : aTable = a := by decide +kernel

private theorem bTable_eq : bTable = b := by decide +kernel

/-- The ten tables lie in `M22`. -/
private theorem tables_mem :
    aTable ∈ M22 ∧ bTable ∈ M22 ∧ g1 ∈ M22 ∧ h1 ∈ M22 ∧ g2 ∈ M22 ∧ h2 ∈ M22 ∧ g3 ∈ M22 ∧
      h3 ∈ M22 ∧ k3 ∈ M22 ∧ g4 ∈ M22 := by
  have ha : aTable ∈ M22 := aTable_eq ▸ a_mem
  have hb : bTable ∈ M22 := bTable_eq ▸ b_mem
  simp only [g1_eq, h1_eq, g2_eq, h2_eq, g3_eq, h3_eq, k3_eq, g4_eq, mul_mem_cancel_right ha,
    mul_mem_cancel_right hb, mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hb), ha, hb, inv_mem hb, and_self]

/-- Representatives along the base `0, 1, 2, 3, 4`: for `i < 5`, the list `transversal i` consists
of elements of `M22`, written as products of at most three of the tables above and their inverses,
that fix `0, …, i - 1` and send `i` to each point of its basic orbit, in increasing order. -/
private def transversal : ℕ → List (Equiv.Perm (Fin 22))
  | 0 => [1, bTable * aTable, bTable * bTable, bTable⁻¹ * aTable, g1 * aTable, aTable * bTable,
      h1⁻¹ * bTable, g4 * aTable, g2 * aTable, aTable * g2 * aTable, h1 * aTable, g2⁻¹ * bTable,
      aTable, h1⁻¹ * bTable⁻¹, h2 * bTable, g1 * bTable⁻¹, g2⁻¹ * aTable, h2⁻¹ * aTable,
      h2 * bTable⁻¹, bTable * h1⁻¹ * bTable⁻¹, bTable⁻¹, bTable]
  | 1 => [1, h1 * g1, h2 * h1⁻¹, g2 * h1, g2⁻¹ * h1, h1⁻¹ * g1, g1 * h1⁻¹, h1⁻¹, h3 * h1, g2 * g1,
      g2⁻¹ * g1, g2⁻¹ * h1⁻¹, g1 * h1, h2 * g1, h1⁻¹ * h1⁻¹, k3⁻¹ * h1⁻¹, h1, h1 * h1, h3⁻¹ * h1,
      h3⁻¹ * g1, g1]
  | 2 => [1, h2⁻¹ * g2, h2⁻¹, g3 * h2⁻¹, h2⁻¹ * h2⁻¹, g2 * h2, h3⁻¹ * g2⁻¹, g2 * h2⁻¹, h2,
      k3⁻¹ * h2⁻¹, g3 * h2, g2, k3 * g2, h3⁻¹ * h2, h3⁻¹ * g2, g2⁻¹ * h2⁻¹, g2⁻¹, g2 * g2,
      h2⁻¹ * g2⁻¹, g2⁻¹ * h2]
  | 3 => [1, g3 * k3⁻¹, k3⁻¹, k3 * h3⁻¹, h3 * g3, h3, k3, g4 * h3⁻¹, g3 * k3, g3 * h3⁻¹, g3,
      h3⁻¹ * k3, k3⁻¹ * h3, h3⁻¹, g4 * g3, g3 * h3]
  | 4 => [1, g4, g4⁻¹]
  | _ => []

private theorem mem_of_mem_transversal {i : ℕ} {r : Equiv.Perm (Fin 22)} (hr : r ∈ transversal i) :
    r ∈ M22 := by
  obtain ⟨ha, hb, hg1, hh1, hg2, hh2, hg3, hh3, hk3, hg4⟩ := tables_mem
  revert r
  match i with
  | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 =>
    simp only [transversal, List.forall_mem_cons, List.not_mem_nil, IsEmpty.forall_iff,
      implies_true, and_true, mul_mem_cancel_right ha, mul_mem_cancel_right hb,
      mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hb), mul_mem_cancel_right hg1, mul_mem_cancel_right hh1,
      mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hh1), mul_mem_cancel_right hg2,
      mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hg2), mul_mem_cancel_right hh2,
      mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hh2), mul_mem_cancel_right hg3, mul_mem_cancel_right hh3,
      mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hh3), mul_mem_cancel_right hk3,
      mul_mem_cancel_right (inv_mem hk3), inv_mem_iff, ha, hb, hg1, hh1, hg2, hh2, hg3, hh3, hk3,
      hg4, one_mem]
  | _ + 5 => simp [transversal]

private theorem mem_transversal : ∀ i < 5, ∀ r ∈ transversal i, r ∈ M22 :=
  fun _ _ _ ↦ mem_of_mem_transversal

private theorem le_closure_tables : M22 ≤ Subgroup.closure {g | g ∈ [aTable, bTable]} := by
  rw [M22_def, aTable_eq, bTable_eq]
  exact Subgroup.closure_mono (by simp [Set.insert_subset_iff])

private theorem transversalCheck_eq_true :
    TauCeti.transversalCheck (Fin.ofNat 22) transversal 5 = true := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem sift_tables :
    [aTable, bTable].all (TauCeti.sift (Fin.ofNat 22) transversal 0 5 1) = true := by
  decide +kernel

/- The sifting conditions are checked one level at a time: together they are thousands of sifts,
too many for a single kernel check within the default heartbeat budget. -/

private theorem siftCheck_0 : TauCeti.siftCheck (Fin.ofNat 22) transversal 5 0 = true := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem siftCheck_1 : TauCeti.siftCheck (Fin.ofNat 22) transversal 5 1 = true := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem siftCheck_2 : TauCeti.siftCheck (Fin.ofNat 22) transversal 5 2 = true := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem siftCheck_3 : TauCeti.siftCheck (Fin.ofNat 22) transversal 5 3 = true := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem siftCheck_4 : TauCeti.siftCheck (Fin.ofNat 22) transversal 5 4 = true := by
  decide +kernel

private theorem siftCheck_eq_true :
    ∀ i < 5, TauCeti.siftCheck (Fin.ofNat 22) transversal 5 i = true := by
  intro i hi
  interval_cases i
  exacts [siftCheck_0, siftCheck_1, siftCheck_2, siftCheck_3, siftCheck_4]

/-- **The points `0, 1, 2, 3, 4` form a base of `M22`**: no element of `M22` other than `1` fixes
all of them. -/
theorem stabilizerChain_five_eq_bot : M22.stabilizerChain (Fin.ofNat 22) 5 = ⊥ :=
  Subgroup.stabilizerChain_eq_bot_of_siftCheck mem_transversal le_closure_tables
    transversalCheck_eq_true sift_tables siftCheck_eq_true

end M22

/-- **The order of `M₂₂`.** The permutation model `M22` has `443520 = 22 * 21 * 20 * 16 * 3`
elements: along the base `0, 1, 2, 3, 4` its basic orbits have lengths `22`, `21`, `20`, `16` and
`3`. -/
theorem card_M22 : Nat.card M22 = 443520 := by
  rw [Subgroup.natCard_eq_prod_length_of_siftCheck M22.mem_transversal M22.le_closure_tables
    M22.transversalCheck_eq_true M22.sift_tables M22.siftCheck_eq_true]
  decide

/-- The group presented by the presentation of `M₂₂`, when finite, has at least `443520` elements,
since it maps onto the model `M22`. Finiteness is a hypothesis: with the junk value `Nat.card = 0`
for infinite types, a surjection alone gives no lower bound. -/
theorem le_natCard_group_M22 (h : 0 < Nat.card SporadicName.M22.Group) :
    443520 ≤ Nat.card SporadicName.M22.Group := by
  have := Nat.finite_of_card_ne_zero h.ne'
  obtain ⟨f, hf⟩ := exists_surjective_M22
  exact card_M22 ▸ Nat.card_le_card_of_surjective f hf

end TauCeti.Sporadic
